course: dictionary
lesson: 14248
title: Negligible: too small to matter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Negligible means so small or unimportant that you can ignore it. Think of it as "not worth counting". When you meet this word, ask yourself: is the thing really big enough to change the answer or the outcome? If not, it is negligible.
explain: «The mistake had a negligible effect on the final score.» The score hardly changed, so the mistake did not matter much. A negligible amount is hardly anything at all.
explain: Scientists and mathematicians use negligible in a special way. «The air resistance on a small, heavy ball is negligible, so we can leave it out of the sum.» Leaving out a negligible value keeps the calculation simple without making the answer wrong.
fact: Negligible means so small or unimportant that it is not worth thinking about.
fact: «A negligible amount» means a very small amount, hardly anything at all.
fact: In maths and science, negligible means small enough to be ignored in a calculation.
fact: Common phrases are negligible effect, negligible risk and negligible amount.
fact: Negligible is a formal word, so you will often see it in exams and in science reports.
exercise: «The price rose by a negligible amount this year.» What does negligible mean here? | Easy to see from far away | Sudden and surprising | Very large and important | *Very small, hardly worth counting
exercise: «The air resistance on the small ball was negligible, so we ignored it.» What does this tell you? | It doubled the final number | *It was too small to change the answer | It stopped the ball completely | Air resistance was the biggest force
exercise: Fill the gap: «The effect of one drop of water on the full bath was ___.» | gigantic | loud | tasty | *negligible
test: «The cost of the paper clip was negligible compared to the whole project.» What does negligible mean? | Unfair to everyone | *Very small, not worth thinking about | Very expensive | Hard to find
test: A scientist says, «The friction is negligible.» What does she mean? | It is unknown and must be measured | *It is so small we can ignore it | It is the most important part of the test | It is very large and must be counted
exam: «Because the error was negligible, the answer is still correct.» Which word best replaces negligible? | bright | *tiny | enormous | wet
